   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Wrist & Hand · I. Bones of the Wrist & Hand",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = notes only · ○ = book only · plain bullets = notes and book*\n\n## 1.1 QUICK FACTS & THE WRIST COMPLEX\n- 27 bones = 8 carpals + 5 metacarpals + 14 phalanges\n- 2 hand functions: power grip (static) and precision handling (finger manipulation)\n- Joints in hand (excluding wrist): CMC 5 + MCP 5 + IP 9 (8 fingers + 1 thumb) = 19\n- ○ 19 bones and 19 joints distal to the carpals make up the hand complex; each digit has a CMC and an MP joint, the fingers have 2 IP joints (PIP and DIP), the thumb has 1\n- Wrist ROM norms: flexion 80°, extension 70°, radial deviation 20°, ulnar deviation 30°\n- ○ Normal ranges vary: 65° to 85° flexion, 60° to 85° extension, 15° to 21° radial deviation, 20° to 45° ulnar deviation\n- ★ Wrist capsular pattern: equal limitation of flexion and extension\n- ○ The wrist (carpus) = two compound joints: radiocarpal and midcarpal, together the wrist complex\n- ○ Wrist complex is biaxial: flexion/extension around a coronal axis; radial/ulnar deviation around an anteroposterior axis\n- ○ Major contribution of the wrist: controls length-tension relationships in the multiarticular hand muscles and allows fine adjustment of grip; it serves hand placement in space only to a minor degree\n- ○ Why two joints instead of one: (1) large ROM with less exposed articular surface and tighter capsules, (2) less structural pinch at extremes, (3) flatter surfaces better withstand pressure\n- ○ Nomenclature: flexion/extension = volar (palmar) flexion/dorsiflexion; radial/ulnar deviation = abduction/adduction; volar = palmar; posterior hand = dorsum CARPALS, LATERAL TO MEDIAL, PROXIMAL TO DISTAL Row Bones Notes\n- Proximal Scaphoid, Lunate, Triquetrum, Pisiform Scaphoid = most fractured. Lunate = most dislocated. Pisiform = sesamoid in FCU tendon.\n- Distal Trapezium, Trapezoid, Capitate, Hamate Capitate = largest. Trapezium = saddle joint for thumb. Hamate has the hook.\n- ★ Class mnemonic: She Looks Too Pretty, Try To Catch Her\n- ★ Typed mnemonic: Stop Letting Those People Touch The Cadaver's Hand\n- ○ The proximal carpal row = scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum; the pisiform is anatomically part of it but does not take part in the radiocarpal joint; it works entirely as a sesamoid that increases the moment arm of the FCU\n- ○ Distal row (trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate) with its metacarpals moves as an almost fixed unit; the capitate and hamate are most strongly bound together\n- ○ The head of the capitate is the “keystone” of the wrist, proposed location of the coronal axis (flexion/extension) and A-P axis (radial/ulnar deviation) and the rigid center of the fixed carpal arch\n- ○ The proximal carpal row and its two interosseous ligaments form a single biconvex, cartilage-covered surface that can change shape somewhat to fit the space between forearm and hand\n## 1.2 FOREARM, METACARPALS & PHALANGES\n- Distal radius: styloid (lateral), ulnar notch (medial), Lister's (dorsal) tubercle (posterior)\n- Distal ulna: head and styloid. The head is separated from the triquetrum by the articular disc (TFCC).\n- Metacarpal 1 is the shortest and most mobile, rotated 90°. Each metacarpal has a base, shaft and head.\n- Phalanges: thumb has 2, digits 2 to 5 have 3.\n- Radius articulates with the scaphoid and lunate. Ulna does not touch the carpals directly.\n- ○ Radiocarpal joint = radius + radioulnar disc (part of the TFCC) proximally; scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum distally\n- ○ Proximal surface has 3 parts: lateral radial facet → scaphoid; medial radial facet → lunate; TFCC → mainly triquetrum (some lunate contact in neutral)\n- ○ Distal radius is biconcave: long and shallow side to side, shorter and sharper anteroposteriorly\n- ○ Radial inclination 23° (radial side 12 mm longer than ulnar side); volar tilt 11° (posterior radius slightly longer than volar)\n- ○ Carpal surface is more sharply curved than the radius → incongruent joint: only about 20% of the surface is in contact (never more than 40%)\n- ○ Incongruence + angulation → greater flexion than extension and greater ulnar than radial deviation at the radiocarpal joint; total flexion/extension range > radial/ulnar deviation range\n- ○ Axial load: scaphoid + lunate take about 80%, TFCC about 20%; at the distal radius, 60% of contact is with the scaphoid and 40% with the lunate\n- ○ Metacarpals: M2 articulates mainly with trapezoid (also trapezium, capitate) and with M3 but not M1; M3 with capitate; M4 with capitate and hamate; M5 with hamate\n## 1.3 TFCC & ULNAR VARIANCE\n- ○ TFCC = radioulnar disc + fibrous attachments: primary support of the distal radioulnar joint; at the wrist it works as an extension of the distal radius\n- ○ The articular disc is a fibrocartilaginous continuation of the distal radius cartilage; medially it connects through two laminae\n- ○ Upper lamina: dorsal and volar radioulnar ligaments → ulnar head and ulnar styloid. Lower lamina: ECU sheath, triquetrum, hamate and base of 5th metacarpal (via ulnar collateral ligament)\n- ○ Meniscus homolog: irregular connective tissue in the lower lamina, from the dorsal radius to the triquetrum; contributes to the prestyloid recess\n- ○ Ulnar negative variance = short ulna; ulnar positive variance = long ulna\n- ○ Positive variance → thinner TFCC and possible impingement of the TFCC between distal ulna and triquetrum; pain at end-range pronation and ulnar deviation; treatment can be ulnar shortening\n- ○ Negative variance → abnormal force across the radiocarpal joint and degeneration; associated with Kienböck's disease (AVN of the lunate); treat by lengthening the ulna, shortening the radius or fusing select carpals\n- ○ Distal radius fracture from FOOSH that heals short: loss of radial length and inclination, reversed volar tilt (dorsal angulation), relatively long ulna → loss of ROM, joint degeneration; may need ORIF with plate and screws\n## 1.4 PALPATION\n- ★ Palpate carpals best from the dorsum. Proximal landmarks: ulnar styloid, Lister's tubercle, radial styloid.\n- ★ Path: radial styloid → scaphoid → lunate → triquetrum → pisiform (volar, in front of the ulna) → hamate and hook of hamate (1 fingerbreadth distal and lateral to the pisiform). The ulnar nerve runs by the hook.\n- ★ Abductor digiti minimi is easily palpated at the hypothenar eminence.\n- ★ Radial styloid sits about 0.75 in. distal to the ulnar styloid.\n- ★ Anterior wrist, lateral to medial: radial pulse → FCR → PL (over the median nerve) → FCU → pisiform. The ulnar pulse and nerve lie just lateral to FCU.\n- ★ Skin creases: proximal crease = wrist joint. Distal crease = proximal border of the flexor retinaculum.\n- ★ Tight muscle → tight flexor retinaculum → narrower tunnel.",
   "order": 1,
   "updated": 1794880000000
