   "title": "Wrist & Hand · III. Critical Spaces",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = notes only · ○ = book only · plain bullets = notes and book*\n\n## 3.1 CARPAL TUNNEL\n- Floor: carpal bone concavity. Roof: flexor retinaculum.\n- 10 contents: median nerve + 4 FDS + 4 FDP + 1 FPL (9 tendons + 1 nerve).\n- FDS and FDP are in the common ulnar bursa. FPL is in the radial bursa.\n- ★ FCR runs in its own split compartment of the retinaculum, so it is considered part of the tunnel but is not a content.\n- CTS: median nerve compression. You get paresthesia in the lateral 3.5 digits, thenar atrophy, and spared palm sensation. The little finger is also spared because Guyon's canal is separate.\n- Tests: Phalen's, Tinel's, Durkan's (carpal compression). The palmar cutaneous branch leaves before the tunnel and runs over the retinaculum, which is why the thenar skin is spared.\n- Treatment goal is to decompress. Surgical release cuts the retinaculum.\n- ○ Tunnel = the proximal transverse (carpal) arch kept by the transverse carpal ligament (distal part of the flexor retinaculum) + transverse intercarpal ligaments; the proximal retinaculum is continuous with forearm fascia\n- ○ The flexor retinaculum prevents bowstringing of the long flexor tendons, preserving length-tension\n- ○ Proximal edge of the TCL = most common site of flexion-induced compression; the tunnel is narrowest at the hook of the hamate (compression there is unaffected by wrist position)\n- ○ After TCL release the arch widens a little but keeps its dorsovolar stiffness if the transverse intercarpal ligaments are intact\n- ○ Case: nocturnal numbness of thumb, index, middle and radial half of ring finger; Tinel's (tapping over the tunnel) and Phalen's (sustained wrist flexion for 1 minute) positive; night splint in neutral; nerve conduction slowing; long-term thenar atrophy = “ape hand” (flat palm, adducted thumb)\n## 3.2 ULNAR TUNNEL (GUYON'S CANAL)\n- ★ Ulnar nerve + artery pass superficial to the retinaculum, lateral to the pisiform.\n- ★ Impingement compromises the nerve and the blood supply to the distal fingers. In a growing child this means poor blood supply and deformity.\n- ★ Causes: hook of hamate fracture, cycling (handlebar palsy), ganglion cyst.\n## 3.3 ANATOMICAL SNUFFBOX & EXTENSOR RETINACULUM\n- ★ Lateral (anterior) border: APL + EPB. Medial (posterior) border:\n### EPL.\n- ★ Floor: radial styloid, scaphoid, trapezium, base of MC1.\n- ★ Contents: radial artery, superficial branch of the radial nerve, origin of the cephalic vein.\n- ★ Tenderness here = suspect scaphoid fracture.\n- ★ Texting thumb / de Quervain's: inflamed APL and EPB tendons. Painful thumb tendons are tested by tucking the thumb in the fist and ulnar deviating (Finkelstein).\n- Extensor retinaculum: 6 dorsal compartments (lateral to medial) # Tendons\n- 1 APL + EPB (de Quervain's)\n- 2 ECRL + ECRB\n- 3 EPL (hooks around Lister's tubercle)\n- 4 ED + EI\n- 5 EDM (isolated)\n- 6 ECU (isolated)\n- ○ Septa of the retinaculum attach to the dorsal carpal ligaments, keep the extensor tendons stable and prevent bowstringing; each tendon has its own sheath, ending soon after the tendon leaves the retinaculum\n- ○ EPL uses Lister's (dorsal radial) tubercle as an anatomical pulley to turn toward the thumb\n## 3.4 PALMAR & OTHER SPACES\n- ★ Thenar space: deep to the index flexors, in front of adductor pollicis. Contains the 1st lumbrical.\n- ★ Midpalmar space: behind the flexors of digits 3 to 5, in front of the interossei. Contains lumbricals 2 to 4.\n- ★ Space of Parona: forearm, between FDP and pronator quadratus. Pus from ruptured bursae collects here.\n- ★ Felon: infection of the finger pulp space. Rising pressure can lead to avascular necrosis of the phalanx diaphysis.\n- ★ Lymph from the palm drains to the dorsum, so palmar infections cause dorsal swelling.\n- ★ Kanavel's 4 signs of flexor tenosynovitis: fusiform swelling, flexed posture, tenderness along the sheath, pain on passive extension.\n- ★ Radial and ulnar bursae can communicate, giving a “horseshoe” abscess.\n## 3.5 FLEXOR GLIDING MECHANISMS\n- ○ Gliding mechanism = flexor retinacula, bursae and digital tendon sheaths: tether the tendons without friction\n- ○ Ulnar bursa invests all 8 FDS/FDP tendons (compartmentalized); radial bursa invests FPL and is continuous with its digital sheath. The ulnar bursa is continuous with the little-finger sheath in only about 30% of specimens\n- ○ For index, middle and ring fingers the ulnar bursa ends just distal to the proximal palmar crease and the sheaths begin at the middle/distal palmar creases\n- ○ Infection travels the full length of a digital sheath (painful tenosynovitis); if the sheath is continuous with a bursa it can spread into the palm. Sheaths end at the distal middle phalanx, so fingertip pad punctures rarely infect the sheath\n- ○ Pulleys: 5 annular (A1 to A5) + 3 cruciate (C1 to C3); A1 at the metacarpal head (floor = deep transverse metacarpal ligament), A2 on the proximal phalanx midshaft, A3 distal proximal phalanx, A4 middle phalanx. A5 is at the base of the distal phalanx. An extra palmar aponeurosis (PA) pulley lies proximal to A1. Thumb: 2 annular + 1 oblique\n- ○ A2 and A4 are the most critical pulleys for flexor efficiency and should be salvaged in tendon reconstruction. Pulleys keep tendons close to bone (minimal bowstringing)\n- ○ Camper's chiasma: the FDS splits and the FDP passes through it; FDS rejoins to insert on the middle phalanx. A4, A5, C3 contain only the FDP\n- ○ Vincula tendinum (usually 4): synovial folds carrying blood vessels to the tendon body and insertions; tendons are also nourished by synovial fluid\n- ○ Trigger finger: repetitive trauma → nodule on the tendon + thickened annular pulley; the nodule catches beneath the pulley in active flexion and needs passive extension to unlock\n- ○ MCP flexor layers, deep to superficial: volar plate, capsule, deep transverse metacarpal ligament (grooved), FDP, FDS, digital sheath, A1 pulley (roof)",
   "order": 3,
   "updated": 1794880000000
