   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Back · III. Joints, Discs and Ligaments",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture & slides · ○ = standard anatomy added · plain bullets = book & teacher's notes*\n\n### CRANIOVERTEBRAL JOINTS\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Transverse ligament keeps the dens against the atlas ring.\n- – If it tears, the dens pokes into the cord.\n- ★ Above C2–C3 the posterior longitudinal ligament becomes the membrana tectoria\n- – above C7 the supraspinous ligament becomes the ligamentum nuchae.\n### FIG 12.4: UPPER CERVICAL JOINTS\n- A, B atlanto-occipital joints, front and back views.\n- C, D atlantoaxial joints: sagittal section and posterior view with the posterior arch of the atlas removed.\n- ★ Know the transverse band of the cruciate ligament holding the dens. Joint Type / movement Ligaments Atlanto\u0002occipital 2 synovial joints: occipital condyles on atlas lateral masses. Flexion, extension, lateral flexion; NO rotation. Enclosed by a capsule Anterior membrane (continuation of ALL, anterior arch to foramen magnum); posterior membrane (like ligamentum flavum, posterior arch to foramen magnum) Atlantoaxial 3 synovial joints: dens with anterior arch of atlas + 2 between lateral masses. Extensive rotation of atlas and head on axis. Enclosed by capsules Apical (tip of dens to foramen magnum), alar (dens to medial occipital condyles; check head rotation), cruciate (transverse part + vertical part), membrana tectoria\n- Cruciate ligament — transverse part binds the dens to the anterior arch of the atlas (attached to the inner side of each lateral mass)\n- – vertical part runs from the back of the axis body to the foramen magnum.\n- ○ The cruciate ligament = the transverse ligament (horizontal band holding the dens against the anterior arch of the atlas) + a superior longitudinal band going up to the basilar part of the occipital bone (front of the foramen magnum) + an inferior longitudinal band going down to the body of the axis (C2).\n- – Together they form the cross (hence “cruciate”); the two bands are the book’s “vertical part”.\n- Membrana tectoria — upward continuation of the posterior longitudinal ligament, attached inside the foramen magnum\n- – covers the dens, apical, alar and cruciate ligaments.\n- In the lower cervical region, small synovial joints lie at the sides of the disc between adjacent bodies.\n### TYPICAL JOINTS BELOW THE AXIS\n- Between bodies — cartilaginous joints.\n- – Thin hyaline plates cover the body surfaces with an intervertebral disc of fibrocartilage between them.\n- Between arches — plane synovial joints between superior and inferior articular processes (hyaline cartilage, capsular ligament).\n### THE INTERVERTEBRAL DISC\n### LECTURE\n- ★ 23 intervertebral discs in the whole column: 6 cervical (C2–C3 to C7–T1), 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar (down to L5–S1).\n- ★ Disc thickness relative to body height dictates movement — a taller disc on a smaller body tilts more (like a ball between two plates).\n- – Height itself matters less than how much you move.\n- ★ Build changes the ratio: a long-legged person has a relatively shorter column (smaller disc-to-column ratio)\n- – a short-legged person has a relatively longer column (bigger ratio).\n- ★ If a disc collapses (heavy movement or loading) it can impinge the spinal nerve root or cord.\n- ★ Annulus is the fibrous part, lots of type I collagen (mostly the outer part ○)\n- – nucleus is the gelatinous part, lots of type II collagen.\n- – Vertebral end plate is cartilaginous.\n- Discs are the main structures binding the bodies; they run from C2 to the sacrum (C1 has no body).\n- – No disc between C1–C2, in the sacrum or in the coccyx.\n- Responsible for about ¼ of column length below C2; thickest in cervical and lumbar where movement is greatest.\n- Uncovertebral joints (joints of Luschka) — in C3–C7, small synovial joints between the uncinate processes and the bevelled edge of the body above\n- – osteophytes here can grow into the intervertebral foramen and press the exiting cervical nerve.\n- With age the discs lose height, so normal loss of stature follows.\n- Shock absorber when load rises suddenly (jumping from a height); elasticity lets rigid bodies move on each other.\n- – Resilience is lost with age.\n- Anulus fibrosus (outer) — fibrocartilage, collagen in concentric sheets running obliquely, direction reversed in alternate sheets\n- – outer fibers firmly attached to the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments.\n- – Canvas notes: about 14 concentric lamellae, fibers at alternating ~65° angles in adjacent layers (onion-skin pattern), thicker in front and at the sides.\n- Nucleus pulposus (center) — in the young, an oval gelatinous mass (lots of water, few fibers, few cells), under pressure, set slightly nearer the posterior margin.\n- – Canvas notes: avascular gelatinous mucopolysaccharide matrix, ~80%+ water, a remnant of the notochord\n- – deforms under load to spread compression evenly.\n- Function — the semifluid nucleus changes shape so one vertebra can rock forward or back (flexion/extension).\n- – Sudden compression flattens the nucleus; the anulus absorbs the outward thrust but can rupture.\n- Aging — the nucleus dries out (desiccation, loses its hydration) and is replaced by rigid fibrocartilage\n- – the anulus lamellae develop fissures\n- – discs lose elasticity, shrink in height and absorb shock less\n- – in old age nucleus and anulus can no longer be told apart.\n### LIGAMENTS OF THE COLUMN\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Six major ligaments — anterior longitudinal, posterior longitudinal, ligamentum flavum, interspinous, supraspinous, intertransverse.\n- ★ Ligamentum nuchae is the thickest and most superficial cervical ligament; you can feel it as firm tissue in the midline below the occiput. Ligament Where Notes Anterior longitudinal (ALL) Front of bodies, skull to sacrum Wide; strongly attached to bodies, sides and discs; continues up as the anterior atlanto-occipital membrane Posterior longitudinal (PLL) Back of bodies, skull to sacrum Weak and narrow; attached to disc edges. ★ Thick enough to shield the cord, so most herniations go to the side. Becomes the tectorial membrane above C2 Ligamentum flavum Between adjacent laminae\n- ★ Elastic, keeps constant tension between vertebrae; not pain sensitive (no nerve supply, as heard in the lecture). Needle passes through it in a spinal tap Supraspinous Tips of adjacent spines\n- ★ Above C7 it is replaced by the ligamentum nuchae Interspinous Between adjacent spines In the neck thickened into the nuchae Intertransverse Between transverse processes\n- ★ The 6th of the six major ligaments\n- All hold the vertebrae firmly yet permit a small amount of movement between them.\n- Ligamentum nuchae — thickened supraspinous + interspinous ligaments from the spine of C7 to the external occipital protuberance\n- – the front edge is firmly attached to the cervical spines.\n- What each ligament resists — ALL = hyperextension; PLL = hyperflexion\n- – ligamentum flavum = hyperflexion, and its elastic recoil helps keep the upright posture and stops the dura being pinched\n- – supraspinous (C7 to sacrum), interspinous and nuchae = hyperflexion\n- – intertransverse = lateral flexion to the opposite side.\n### NERVE SUPPLY OF THE JOINTS\n### FIG 12.6: INNERVATION OF VERTEBRAL\n### JOINTS\n- Meningeal branch of each spinal nerve re-enters the canal and supplies meninges, ligaments and discs.\n- Posterior rami supply the joints between articular processes.\n- Also shown: anterior ramus, white and gray rami communicantes, sympathetic trunk.\n- Joints between bodies: small meningeal branches arise as the spinal nerve leaves the intervertebral foramen, re-enter through it and supply meninges, ligaments and discs.\n- Joints between articular processes: branches of the posterior rami.\n- Each level receives fibers from two adjacent spinal nerves (overlap).",
