   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Back · IV. Curves and Movements",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture & slides · ○ = standard anatomy added · plain bullets = book & teacher's notes*\n\n### CURVES IN THE SAGITTAL PLANE\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Primary curves are kyphotic (convex backward) and present in the newborn: thoracic and sacrococcygeal.\n- ★ Secondary curves are lordotic (concave backward) and develop after birth as posture is learned: cervical (head control) and lumbar (trunk control).\n- ★ Postural faults you will meet in assessment: slouched posture, forward head posture, scoliosis (sway back and flat back also named ○).\n- The fetal column is one continuous anterior concavity; the lumbosacral angle appears as development proceeds. Age / milestone What happens Newborn Whole column concave anteriorly (primary kyphotic curve); lumbosacral angle forming\n- ★ 3–4 months Baby holds head up steadily, so the cervical curve becomes concave posteriorly (lordosis)\n- ★ 8–10 months to end of 1st year Pulls up on the crib and stands upright, trunk extends, so the lumbar curve forms (lordosis) Adult Cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, lumbar lordosis, sacral kyphosis Late pregnancy Women increase the lumbar concavity to keep the center of gravity Old age Discs atrophy, height lost, column drifts back toward one continuous anterior concavity\n- Development of the secondary curves changes the shape of the vertebral bodies and discs.\n### CORONAL PLANE AND ABNORMAL CURVES\n- Late childhood often brings minor lateral thoracic curves, from using one upper limb more: right-handed people have a slight right thoracic convexity\n- – small compensatory curves lie above and below. Curve problem Meaning and causes Kyphosis Exaggerated thoracic curve. Weak muscle tone (round shoulders from long hours at a low desk); crush fracture or TB (Pott’s disease) gives acute angular kyphosis (gibbus deformity); osteoporosis / disc degeneration gives senile kyphosis (cervical, thoracic, lumbar) Lordosis Exaggerated lumbar curve. Heavy abdomen (pregnancy, large ovarian tumor), spondylolisthesis; may be compensating for thoracic kyphosis or hip disease (congenital dislocation) Scoliosis Lateral deviation, most often thoracic. Muscle or vertebral defects, polio paralysis, congenital hemivertebra; often compensatory for a short leg or hip disease\n- In a herniated disc, the column leans into a scoliosis with its concavity on the side of the lesion because of muscle spasm.\n- Scoliosis is usually accompanied by rotation of the vertebrae (rib hump on forward bending)\n- – most cases are idiopathic, appear in the adolescent growth spurt, and are more common in females.\n- A spastic muscle feels harder and shorter than normal, producing a concavity of the column on that side.\n### MOVEMENTS OF THE COLUMN\n- Flexion, extension, lateral flexion, rotation, circumduction (combination of all).\n- Flexion and extension are extensive in cervical and lumbar, restricted in thoracic\n- – lateral flexion likewise\n- – rotation is least extensive in lumbar.\n- Range depends on disc thickness and the shape and direction of the articular processes\n- – in the thorax ribs, costal cartilages and sternum severely restrict it.\n- Atlanto-occipital joints: extensive flexion/extension of the head.\n- – Atlantoaxial joints: wide rotation of the head.\n- Circumduction is extensive in the cervical and lumbar regions (lumbar through pelvic tilting) and limited in the thoracic\n- – thoracic rotation is possible but limited.\n- Cervical facets slope at about 45°, which permits movement in all planes; lateral flexion is also called abduction. Region Movement Muscles Cervical Flexion Longus cervicis, scalenus anterior, sternocleidomastoid Extension Postvertebral muscles Lateral flexion Scalenus anterior and medius, trapezius, sternocleidomastoid Rotation Sternocleidomastoid (one side) + splenius (other side) Thoracic Rotation Unilateral semispinalis and rotatores, helped by abdominal obliques Lumbar Flexion Rectus abdominis, psoas Extension Postvertebral muscles Lateral flexion Postvertebral, quadratus lumborum, abdominal obliques (psoas may help) Rotation Rotatores + abdominal obliques EXAMINING THE BACK\n- Expose the whole back and legs, shoes off: unequal leg length or hip disease can distort the curves\n- – watch the patient walk to see pelvic tilt.\n- When one side of the pelvis rises, a lumbar convexity develops on the opposite side with a compensatory thoracic convexity on the same side.\n- Sitting flattens the lumbar curve and widens the gaps between lumbar spines.\n- Cervical — flexion until chin touches chest\n- – extension to look straight up\n- – rotation brings the chin nearly in line with the shoulder\n- – lateral flexion about 45° each side (do not let the shoulder rise).\n- About half of neck flexion is at the atlanto-occipital joints; about half of rotation is between atlas and axis.\n- Thoracic: movements limited by ribs and sternum; when testing rotation make sure the pelvis does not turn.\n- Lumbar: flexion and extension fairly free\n- – rotation limited by interlocking articular processes\n- – test lateral flexion by sliding each hand down the thigh.",
