   "id": "n-ga-015",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Back · VI. Muscles of the Back",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture & slides · ○ = standard anatomy added · plain bullets = book & teacher's notes*\n\n### LAYERS\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Trapezius, rhomboids and other shoulder muscles are classed as superficial back muscles, though their action is mostly on the scapula.\n- ★ Shoulder-complex muscles fall into 3 groups: scapula → vertebral column (still counted as superficial back muscles), scapula → humerus, and trunk → humerus (not attached to the scapula).\n- ★ Back muscles are easy to palpate in a fit person (surface anatomy).\n- ★ Thoracolumbar fascia keeps back muscles firm; if over-stretched and weak, muscles cannot contract well.\n- – It also serves as an attachment for the abdominal muscles.\n- ★ Erector spinae mnemonic: “I Love Spaghetti” = Iliocostalis, Longissimus, Spinalis, lateral to medial (outside going in, superficial to deep).\n- – Iliocostalis: cervicis, thoracis, lumborum.\n- – Longissimus: capitis, cervicis, thoracis (no lumborum).\n- – Spinalis: thoracis and cervicis.\n- ★ Erector spinae = the prominent “pork chop” bulge on each side of the spine.\n- ★ Slide layering of the intrinsic muscles (figs 3.8–3.11) — superficial intrinsic = serratus posterior (superior, inferior) + splenius (capitis, cervicis)\n- – intermediate intrinsic = erector spinae\n- – deep intrinsic = transversospinalis.\n- ★ Family names tell the region — e.g., iliocostalis thoracis = only the thoracic part.\n- ★ Semispinalis has three parts: capitis, cervicis, thoracis.\n- – Multifidus runs the whole column and is the strongest of the transversospinalis\n- – rotatores (breves = short, longi = long) are the smallest and most diagonal.\n- ★ Transversospinalis = core muscles — they keep the lumbar spine stiff even if the erector spinae are weak\n- – multifidus is the most active.\n- – They are stabilizers, not mobilizers.\n- – Transversospinalis run from transverse process to spinous process; the erector spinae attach longitudinally.\n- Three groups — (1) superficial muscles of the shoulder girdle\n- – (2) intermediate muscles that move the thoracic cage\n- – (3) deep (postvertebral) muscles of the column itself.\n- Superficial extrinsic group — trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, rhomboid major and minor.\n- Intermediate extrinsic group — serratus posterior superior and inferior (thin muscles that move the thoracic cage in breathing).\n- All deep back muscles are supplied by the posterior rami of the spinal nerves.\n- Why the supply differs — intrinsic back muscles come from the epimere (dorsal myotome) and keep posterior rami\n- – extrinsic muscles (rhomboids, latissimus dorsi) come from the hypomere (ventral myotome) and are supplied by anterior rami\n- – trapezius is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI).\n### FIG 12.9: DEEP MUSCLES AND LINE OF\n### GRAVITY\n- A erector spinae columns and the transversospinalis (multifidus, semispinalis) on the back.\n- B lateral skeleton: the line of gravity passes through the dens, behind the hip centers, in front of knee and ankle. Layer Muscles Notes Superficial, vertical Erector spinae: iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis\n- ★ Longest muscles; sacrum to rib angles, transverse processes and upper spines Intermediate, oblique Transversospinalis: semispinalis, multifidus, rotatores\n- ★ Transverse process up to spine; the core stabilizers Deepest Interspinales, intertransversarii, levatores costarum Shortest fibers, between adjacent spines or transverse processes Detached part Splenius capitis and cervicis Capitis: lower ligamentum nuchae + upper 4 thoracic spines to superior nuchal line and mastoid process. Cervicis: same origin to upper cervical transverse processes\n- Note — the Canvas notes list serratus posterior as intermediate extrinsic (thin muscles of breathing) and put splenius in the intermediate oblique layer\n- – the slide figure groups both as superficial intrinsic.\n- Splenius cervicis (Canvas notes) — arises from the T3–T6 spinous processes and inserts into the transverse processes of C1–C3.\n- Extended mnemonic: “I Love Spaghetti – Some More Ragu” = Iliocostalis, Longissimus, Spinalis – Semispinalis, Multifidus, Rotatores.\n- Deep muscles form a broad thick column in the groove beside the spines from sacrum to skull\n- – each is like a string that extends or rotates vertebrae.\n- – Overlap lets whole regions move smoothly.\n- The line of gravity passes through the dens, behind the hip centers, in front of the knee and ankle, so most weight falls in front of the column\n- – hence the postvertebral muscles are well developed and their postural tone maintains the curves.\n### SUBOCCIPITAL (SHORT NUCHAL) MUSCLES\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Rectus capitis posterior minor and major, obliquus capitis superior and inferior, plus semispinalis capitis nearby.\n- – Capitis muscles attach to the occiput and give small extension movements.\n- ○ Obliquus capitis inferior runs from the spine of C2 to the transverse process of C1, so it does not reach the skull; it rotates the atlas.\n- – (Quiz item)\n### MUSCULAR TRIANGLES\nTriangle Boundaries Why it matters Auscultatory Latissimus dorsi, trapezius, medial border of scapula Breath sounds heard best with a stethoscope; floor is the 6th–7th intercostal space, with little thick muscle Lumbar Latissimus dorsi, posterior border of external oblique, iliac crest Floor is internal oblique; weak spot where a lumbar hernia or deep pus can emerge (Petit’s triangle) Suboccipital Rectus capitis posterior major (superomedial), obliquus capitis superior (superolateral), obliquus capitis inferior (inferolateral). Rectus capitis posterior minor lies medially but is not a boundary Contains the suboccipital nerve (C1 posterior ramus) and the vertebral artery, which winds round the lateral mass of C1, lies in the groove on the posterior arch of C1, then pierces the posterior atlanto-occipital membrane and dura to enter the foramen magnum\n### THORACOLUMBAR (DEEP) FASCIA\n- Lumbar part lies between the iliac crest and 12th rib, a strong aponeurosis\n- – laterally it gives origin to the transversus abdominis (middle fibers) and the internal oblique (upper fibers).\n- Medially it splits into 3 lamellae: posterior covers the deep back muscles and attaches to the lumbar spines\n- – middle attaches to tips of the lumbar transverse processes, lying in front of the deep muscles and behind quadratus lumborum\n- – anterior attaches to the front of the lumbar transverse processes, in front of quadratus lumborum.",
