   "title": "Back · VII. Vessels and Nerves of the Back",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture & slides · ○ = standard anatomy added · plain bullets = book & teacher's notes*\n\n### ARTERIES\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Cervical — heart → aortic arch → external carotid → occipital; subclavian → vertebral\n- – costocervical trunk → deep cervical.\n- ★ Upper thoracic — costocervical trunk → supreme intercostal → 1st and 2nd posterior intercostals.\n- ★ Thoracic — thoracic aorta → lower nine posterior intercostals.\n- ★ Lumbar — abdominal aorta → 5 lateral abdominal wall branches → 4 lumbar arteries (plus the subcostal).\n- ★ Sacral — abdominal aorta → common iliac → internal iliac (posterior division) → iliolumbar and lateral sacral.\n### VEINS AND THE VERTEBRAL PLEXUSES\n### FIG 12.10: OBLIQUE SECTION THROUGH\nL1\n- Cord with its three coverings inside the canal; spinal nerve splits into anterior and posterior rami.\n- Internal vertebral venous plexus lies outside the dura; basivertebral vein drains the body into it.\n- Sympathetic trunk and ganglion lie in front, linked by white and gray rami.\n- Veins form plexuses along the whole column, skull to coccyx: external vertebral plexus (outside the column) and internal vertebral plexus (inside the canal, outside the dura, in the epidural space).\n- The internal vertebral venous plexus is also called Batson’s plexus.\n- Thin walled, valveless or with incompetent valves\n- – they communicate through the foramen magnum with the skull's venous sinuses, so blood flows freely between skull, neck, thorax, abdomen, pelvis and plexus, depending on pressure.\n- The internal plexus gets tributaries from the vertebrae (basivertebral veins), meninges and cord\n- – it drains through intervertebral veins with the spinal nerves into the vertebral, intercostal, lumbar and lateral sacral veins.\n- Clinical — pelvic venous blood enters not only the inferior vena cava but also the vertebral plexus, and by this route may reach the skull, especially when intra-abdominal pressure rises.\n- – The internal plexus is not subject to external pressure when abdominal pressure rises, so blood is forced backward from the abdominal and pelvic veins into the veins in the vertebral canal.\n- – This is how carcinoma of the prostate can metastasize to the vertebrae and the cranial cavity.\n### LYMPH DRAINAGE\nFrom To Deep vessels (follow the veins) Deep cervical, posterior mediastinal, lateral aortic and sacral nodes Skin of the neck Cervical nodes Skin of trunk above iliac crests Axillary nodes Skin of trunk below iliac crests Superficial inguinal nodes\n### NERVE SUPPLY OF THE BACK\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Dermatomes are important — the lecturer included a back dermatome map in his slides.\n- ★ Quiz point: back muscles are supplied by the posterior ramus.\n- Skin and muscles are supplied segmentally by the posterior rami of the 31 pairs of spinal nerves.\n- Posterior rami of C1, C6, C7, C8, L4, L5 supply deep muscles only, not skin.\n- The posterior ramus of C2 = greater occipital nerve, supplying the scalp skin up to the vertex.\n- Posterior rami run downward and laterally to a skin band lower than their foramen\n- – each divides into medial and lateral branches\n- – dermatomes overlap, so cutting one nerve only diminishes sensation.\n### PRINCIPAL DERMATOMES (SLIDE 87)\nLevel Area Level Area C5 Clavicles T12 Inguinal or groin regions C5, 6, 7 Lateral parts of upper limbs L1, 2, 3, 4 Anterior and inner surfaces of lower limbs C8, T1 Medial sides of upper limbs L4, 5, S1 Foot C6 Thumb L4 Medial side of great toe C6, 7, 8 Hand S1, 2, L5 Posterior and outer surfaces of lower limbs C8 Ring and little fingers S1 Lateral margin of foot and little toe T4 Level of nipples S2, 3, 4 Perineum T10 Level of umbilicus",
   "order": 16,
   "updated": 1794880000000
