   "id": "n-ga-017",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Back · VIII. Spinal Cord, Roots and Meninges",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture & slides · ○ = standard anatomy added · plain bullets = book & teacher's notes*\n\n### THE CORD ITSELF\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Lecture: the cord ends around L1–L2; below that the roots run a long way before they exit.\n- ★ Slides: the cord ends at L1 or L2 in adults and just above L3 in young children.\n- ★ Gray matter (butterfly): anterior horn, posterior horn, and the gray commissure around the central canal.\n- ★ White matter — anterior, lateral and posterior funiculi; the anterior commissure lies in front of the gray commissure.\n- ★ Posterior (dorsal) root carries the dorsal root ganglion (sensory); the anterior (ventral) root is motor.\n- Cylindrical gray-white structure from the foramen magnum (continuous with the medulla) to the lower border of L1 in the adult\n- – in a young child it reaches about L3.\n- Cervical and lumbar enlargements (fusiform) give origin to the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses.\n- Tapers into the conus medullaris; the filum terminale (pia extension) descends to the back of the coccyx.\n- Anterior median fissure (deep, front) and posterior median sulcus (shallow furrow, back).\n### FIG 12.11: LOWER CORD, ROOTS,\n### COVERINGS\n- A conus medullaris, filum terminale and cauda equina.\n- B thoracic cord with roots and three meninges; C cross-section showing gray horns, central canal, CSF space.\n### ROOTS AND SPINAL NERVES\n### LECTURE\n- ★ 31 pairs — 8 cervical (C1–C8), 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal.\n- ★ Nerve exit rule: 8 cervical nerves but 7 cervical vertebrae.\n- – C1–C7 exit above their vertebra, C8 below C7; thoracic and lumbar nerves exit below the vertebra of the same number.\n- 31 pairs attach by anterior (motor) and posterior (sensory) roots, each a series of rootlets.\n- – The posterior root has a posterior root ganglion.\n- Roots join at the intervertebral foramen to form a mixed spinal nerve, which immediately splits into a large anterior ramus and a smaller posterior ramus (both mixed).\n- Because the column outgrows the cord, roots get longer from above down: short and nearly horizontal in the upper cervical region\n- – the lumbar and sacral roots below the cord form a vertical leash around the filum terminale, the cauda equina.\n- Each vertebral body is intersegmental, so each spinal nerve leaves through the foramen right beside an intervertebral disc (important for disc prolapse).\n### FIG 12.12: CORD SEGMENTS VS\n### VERTEBRAE\n- Posterior view; on the right the laminae are removed to show the cord and roots.\n- The cord stops at L1, so lower segments lie opposite higher vertebrae.\n### CORD SEGMENT VS VERTEBRAL LEVEL\nVertebra Cord segment opposite Cervical Add 1 Upper thoracic Add 2 Lower thoracic (T7–T9) Add 3 T10 L1 and L2 segments T11 L3 and L4 segments T12 L5 segment L1 Sacral and coccygeal segments\n- Examples: C5 vertebra = C6 segment; T3 = T5; T8 = T11.\n### BLOOD SUPPLY OF THE CORD\n- 3 longitudinal arteries: 2 posterior spinal and 1 anterior spinal.\n- – Posterior spinal arteries arise (directly or indirectly) from the vertebral arteries and run beside the posterior root attachments.\n- The anterior spinal artery is formed by the union of branches of the vertebral arteries and runs in the anterior median fissure.\n- Radicular arteries reinforce them via the intervertebral foramina; cord veins drain to the internal vertebral plexus.\n- Artery of Adamkiewicz (great anterior radicular artery) is the largest feeder: usually on the left, from a lower intercostal or upper lumbar artery at T9–L2\n- – supplies the lumbar enlargement and lower cord.\n- Supply is meager — vessels are small and variable, so ischemia can follow minor arterial damage (regional anesthesia, pain blocks, aortic surgery, aortic clamping), giving anterior spinal artery syndrome or paraplegia.\n### MENINGES AND SPACES\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Quiz point: the cord ends at L1–L2; the subarachnoid space ends at S2. Layer Features Dura mater Dense fibrous outermost sheet around cord and cauda equina; continuous with brain dura at the foramen magnum; ends on the filum terminale at the lower border of S2; extends along each root and merges with the epineurium; lies loose in the canal Arachnoid mater Delicate impermeable membrane between dura and pia; ends at lower border of S2; continues along nerve roots as small lateral extensions of the subarachnoid space Pia mater Vascular membrane closely covering the cord; fuses with the filum terminale; thickened between roots as the ligamentum denticulatum, which attaches to the dura and suspends the cord in the dural sheath Space Contents / notes Extradural (epidural) Between dura and canal wall: loose areolar tissue + internal vertebral venous plexus Subdural Potential space between dura and arachnoid; thin film of tissue fluid Subarachnoid Between arachnoid and pia; filled with CSF; extends down to the lower border of S2\n- Between the conus medullaris and the end of the subarachnoid space, the cauda equina lies bathed in CSF.\n### CEREBROSPINAL FLUID\n- Clear colorless fluid made mainly by choroid plexuses in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles.\n- Leaves the fourth ventricle through 3 foramina in its roof into the subarachnoid space, circulating up over the hemispheres and down around the cord.\n- Returns to blood through arachnoid villi into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus.\n- Removes waste from neuronal activity and gives a fluid cushion that, with the bony and ligamentous canal wall, protects the cord.\n### CORD INJURIES\n- Injury causes partial or complete loss of function at the level and below (ascending and descending tracts).\n- Cervical — large canal, cord often escapes; if displacement is great the cord is severed and death follows.\n- – Respiration stops above the phrenic origin (C3, C4, C5).\n- Thoracic fracture-dislocation — canal is small, displacement large, so severe cord injury.\n- Lumbar — cord ends at L1 and the foramen is large, so cauda equina has room and nerve injury may be minimal.",
