   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Abdomen (Snell) · II. Inguinal Canal",
   "body": "*Key: ◆ = Snell's clinical notes · book only*\n\n### INGUINAL CANAL\n- Oblique passage through the lower anterior abdominal wall\n- – Male → structures pass between the testis and abdomen\n- – Female → the round ligament of the uterus passes from the uterus to the labium majus\n- About 4 cm (1.5 in) long\n- Runs from the deep inguinal ring (hole in the fascia transversalis) down and medially to the superficial inguinal ring (hole in the external oblique aponeurosis)\n- Lies parallel to and just above the inguinal ligament\n- Newborn — deep ring is almost directly behind the superficial ring → canal much shorter; with growth the deep ring moves laterally Inguinal canal layer by layer: external oblique (A), internal oblique (B), transversus (C), fascia transversalis (D)\n### DEEP AND SUPERFICIAL INGUINAL RINGS\nRing Features Deep inguinal ring Oval opening in the fascia transversalis; 1.3 cm (0.5 in) above the inguinal ligament, midway between the ASIS and the symphysis pubis; inferior epigastric vessels lie medial to it; margins attach the internal spermatic fascia Superficial inguinal ring Triangular defect in the external oblique aponeurosis, above and medial to the pubic tubercle; margins (the crura) attach the external spermatic fascia\n- You can't see the rings as openings from outside — the spermatic fasciae are attached to their margins (like the fingers of a glove seen from outside)\n### WALLS OF THE INGUINAL CANAL\nWall Formed by Anterior External oblique aponeurosis, reinforced laterally by the origin of the internal oblique from the inguinal ligament → strongest opposite the deep ring (the weakest part of the posterior wall) Posterior Conjoint tendon medially, fascia transversalis laterally → strongest opposite the superficial ring (the weakest part of the anterior wall) Roof (superior) Arching lowest fibres of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis Floor (inferior) Upturned lower edge of the inguinal ligament; lacunar ligament at the medial end\n### FUNCTION AND MECHANICS OF THE CANAL\n- Lets the spermatic cord pass between the testis and abdomen (spermatogenesis needs the cooler scrotum); in the female the smaller canal carries the round ligament\n- A site of potential weakness in both sexes — how the design helps:\n- – Oblique passage (except in the newborn) → the two rings lie far apart\n- – Anterior wall is reinforced by internal oblique fibres right in front of the deep ring\n- – Posterior wall is reinforced by the conjoint tendon right behind the superficial ring\n- – Coughing / straining — the arched roof (lowest internal oblique and transversus fibres) contracts and flattens down onto the floor, nearly closing the canal\n- – Squatting (defecation, parturition) — thighs flexed; the anterior thighs press against the lower abdominal wall and protect it Muscles closing the canal when they contract (top); squatting protects the groin (bottom)",
