   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Abdomen (Snell) · IV. Posterior Abdominal Wall",
   "body": "*Key: ◆ = Snell's clinical notes · book only*\n\n### STRUCTURE OF THE POSTERIOR ABDOMINAL WALL\n- Midline — 5 lumbar vertebrae and their intervertebral discs\n- Laterally:\n- – 12th ribs\n- – Upper part of the bony pelvis (the iliacus lies in it)\n- – Psoas muscles\n- – Quadratus lumborum muscles\n- – Aponeuroses of origin of the transversus abdominis Costal margin and bones of the abdomen BONES\n- Lumbar vertebra — body massive and kidney-shaped (carries most of the body weight); L5 articulates with the sacral base at the lumbosacral joint\n- Intervertebral discs — thicker in the lumbar region and wedge-shaped → give the lumbar lordosis\n- 12th rib — head has a single facet (articulates with T12 only); anterior end pointed with a small costal cartilage buried in the abdominal wall; often too short to project past the erector spinae\n- Ilium — with the ischium and pubis forms the hip bone (meet at the acetabulum)\n- – Arcuate line divides the medial surface: above = iliac fossa (concave); below = flat surface continuous with pubis and ischium\n- – Arcuate line (posterior) + pectineal line (anterior) = iliopectineal line → separates the false pelvis from the true pelvis Fifth lumbar vertebra Internal aspect of the right hip bone PSOAS MAJOR\n- Origin — roots of the transverse processes, sides of the vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs, T12 to L5\n- Fibres run down and laterally, leave the abdomen behind the inguinal ligament into the thigh\n- Insertion — lesser trochanter of the femur\n- Enclosed in a fibrous sheath from the lumbar fascia; thickened above as the medial arcuate ligament\n- Nerve — lumbar plexus\n- Action — flexes the thigh at the hip; if the thigh is fixed, flexes the trunk (sitting up from lying)\n- Psoas minor — small, long tendon, in front of the major; absent in 40% of people QUADRATUS LUMBORUM\n- Flat quadrilateral muscle alongside the vertebral column\n- Origin — iliolumbar ligament, adjoining iliac crest, tips of the transverse processes of the lower lumbar vertebrae\n- Fibres run up and medially\n- Insertion — lower border of the 12th rib and the transverse processes of the upper 4 lumbar vertebrae\n- Anterior surface covered by lumbar fascia — thickened above as the lateral arcuate ligament, below as the iliolumbar ligament\n- Nerve — lumbar plexus\n- Action — fixes or depresses the 12th rib in respiration; laterally flexes the vertebral column to the same side ILIACUS\n- Fan-shaped; origin — upper part of the iliac fossa\n- Fibres join the lateral side of the psoas tendon → lesser trochanter; the two together = iliopsoas\n- Nerve — femoral nerve (branch of the lumbar plexus)\n- Action — flexes the thigh on the trunk; if the thigh is fixed, flexes the trunk\n- The posterior part of the diaphragm also forms part of the posterior wall Muscles and bones forming the posterior abdominal wall Muscle Psoas Origin Transverse processes, bodies and intervertebral discs of T12 and the 5 lumbar vertebrae Insertion With iliacus into the lesser trochanter of the femur Nerve Lumbar plexus Action Flexes thigh on trunk; if thigh fixed, flexes trunk on thigh (sitting up from lying) Muscle Quadratus lumborum Origin Iliolumbar ligament, iliac crest, tips of transverse processes of lower lumbar vertebrae Insertion 12th rib Nerve Lumbar plexus Action Fixes 12th rib in inspiration; depresses 12th rib in forced expiration; laterally flexes the vertebral column to the same side Muscle Iliacus Origin Iliac fossa Insertion With psoas into the lesser trochanter of the femur Nerve Femoral nerve Action Flexes thigh on trunk; if thigh fixed, flexes trunk on thigh CLINICAL NOTES\n- ◆ Psoas fascia and tuberculosis — the psoas fascia covers the front of the muscle and steers a tuberculous abscess. TB of the thoracolumbar spine destroys vertebral bodies; pus tracks laterally under the psoas fascia, then down along the psoas to appear as a swelling in the upper thigh below the inguinal ligament — can be mistaken for a femoral hernia.\n- – Psoas abscess: swellings in the right groin above and below the inguinal ligament FASCIAL LINING OF THE ABDOMINAL WALLS\n- One continuous connective tissue layer between the parietal peritoneum and the muscles; continuous below with the pelvic wall fascia\n- Named for what it covers: diaphragmatic fascia, transversalis fascia, psoas fascia, quadratus lumborum fascia, iliaca fascia\n- Abdominal blood and lymph vessels lie INSIDE this fascia; the principal nerves lie OUTSIDE\n- Femoral sheath — downward prolongation of the fascial lining around the femoral vessels and lymphatics, ~4 cm (1.5 in) into the thigh behind the inguinal ligament; the femoral nerve lies outside → has no sheath\n- Below the ASIS level the posterior rectus sheath = fascia transversalis + peritoneum only\n- Midway between the ASIS and symphysis pubis the spermatic cord pierces the fascia transversalis → deep inguinal ring; fascia continues over the cord as the internal spermatic fascia\n- – Fascial lining of the abdominal wall (left); femoral sheath, anterior and lateral views (right)\n### PERITONEAL LINING OF THE ABDOMINAL WALLS\n- Parietal peritoneum — thin serous membrane: mesothelium on connective tissue; continuous below with the pelvic peritoneum\n- Nerve supply\n- – Central part of the diaphragmatic peritoneum — phrenic nerves\n- – Peripheral part — lower intercostal nerves\n- – Anterior and posterior abdominal walls — segmentally by intercostal and lumbar nerves (same as the overlying muscle and skin)",
   "order": 40,
   "updated": 1794880000000
