   "title": "Abdomen (Snell) · VI. Surface Anatomy",
   "body": "*Key: ◆ = Snell's clinical notes · book only*\n\n### SURFACE LANDMARKS OF THE ABDOMINAL WALL\nLandmark Key points Xiphoid process Thin cartilaginous lower part of the sternum; felt in the depression where the costal margins meet. Xiphisternal junction is opposite the body of T9 Costal margin Lower curved margin of the thoracic wall: cartilages of ribs 7–10 in front, 11 and 12 behind. Lowest level = 10th costal cartilage (opposite the body of L3). The 12th rib may be short and hard to feel Iliac crest Felt along its whole length; ends at the ASIS (front) and PSIS (behind). Highest point opposite L4. Tubercle of the crest ~5 cm behind the ASIS, at the level of L5 Pubic tubercle Small protuberance on the superior surface of the pubis Symphysis pubis Cartilaginous joint between the pubic bodies; solid in the midline at the lower end of the wall. Pubic crest = ridge medial to the tubercle Inguinal ligament Under the groin skin crease; rolled-under lower margin of the external oblique aponeurosis, ASIS → pubic tubercle Superficial inguinal ring Triangular aperture above and medial to the pubic tubercle. In the male: invaginate the upper scrotal skin with the little finger. Smaller in the female (round ligament) Scrotum Pouch of skin and fascia with the testes, epididymides and lower cords; scrotal raphe = midline fusion line. The testis lies free (not tethered to skin) with the epididymis behind it; vas deferens ascends medial to the epididymis Linea alba Median fibrous band, symphysis pubis → xiphoid; shows as a slight median groove Umbilicus In the linea alba; inconstant position; puckered scar of the cord Rectus abdominis Either side of the linea alba; made prominent by raising the shoulders from the couch without using the arms Tendinous intersections 3 — palpable as transverse depressions at the xiphoid tip, umbilicus and halfway between Linea semilunaris Lateral edge of the rectus; crosses the costal margin at the tip of the 9th costal cartilage Surface landmarks, planes and regions of the anterior abdominal wall\n### ABDOMINAL LINES AND PLANES\nLine / plane Passes through Level Vertical lines (right and left) Midpoint between the ASIS and symphysis pubis — Transpyloric plane Tips of the 9th costal cartilages (where the linea semilunaris crosses the costal margin). Passes through the pylorus, duodenojejunal junction, neck of the pancreas, hila of the kidneys L1 Subcostal plane Lowest point of the costal margin on each side (10th costal cartilage) L3 Intercristal plane Highest points of the iliac crests; used for lumbar puncture L4 Intertubercular plane Tubercles of the iliac crests L5\n- Quadrants — vertical + horizontal line crossing at the umbilicus: upper right, upper left, lower right, lower left\n- Epigastrium = area below the xiphoid and above the umbilicus; periumbilical = around the umbilicus (loosely used)\n### SURFACE LANDMARKS OF THE ABDOMINAL VISCERA\n- Positions vary between people and with posture and respiration; the following are fairly fixed: Organ Surface position Liver Under the lower ribs, most of its bulk on the right. Infants (to ~3 years): lower margin 1–2 fingerbreadths below the costal margin. Adults: not palpable if obese or with a well-developed right rectus; in a thin adult the edge may be felt a fingerbreadth below the margin, easiest at deep inspiration Gallbladder Fundus opposite the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (where the lateral edge of the right rectus crosses the costal margin) Spleen Left upper quadrant, under the 9th, 10th, 11th ribs; long axis = 10th rib; not normally in front of the midaxillary line; lower pole palpable in infants Pancreas Across the transpyloric plane: head below and to the right, neck on the plane, body and tail above and to the left Kidneys Right slightly lower than left (bulk of the right liver lobe); lower pole of the right palpable in the right lumbar region at deep inspiration in weak abdominal muscles; each moves ~2.5 cm with breathing; the normal left is not palpable. Hilum on the transpyloric plane, ~3 fingerbreadths from the midline. On the back, from T12 spine to L3 spine; hila opposite L1 Organ Surface position Stomach Cardioesophageal junction ~3 fingerbreadths below and to the left of the xiphisternal junction (esophagus pierces the diaphragm at T10). Pylorus on the transpyloric plane, just right of the midline. Lesser curvature joins the two. Greater curvature varies — umbilical region or below Duodenum (1st part) On the transpyloric plane, ~4 fingerbreadths right of the midline Cecum Right lower quadrant; often gas-filled (resonant on percussion); palpable Appendix Right lower quadrant; base at McBurney's point = 1/3 of the way up the line from the ASIS to the umbilicus; free end variable Ascending colon Up from the cecum, lateral to the right vertical line, under the right costal margin; palpable Transverse colon Across the umbilical region, arching down (concavity up); position variable (has a mesentery) Descending colon Down from the left costal margin, lateral to the left vertical line; curves medially in the left lower quadrant to the sigmoid; narrower than the ascending; palpable Bladder / pregnant uterus Full bladder and pregnant uterus palpable above the symphysis pubis Aorta Midline; bifurcates at L4 (intercristal plane); pulsations felt through the upper wall just left of the midline External iliac artery Pulse felt under the inguinal ligament, midway between the ASIS and symphysis pubis Surface markings of the liver, gallbladder fundus and spleen (adult and child) Surface anatomy of the kidneys and ureters — front (A) and back (B)",
   "order": 42,
   "updated": 1794880000000
