   "title": "Abdomen (Snell) · XIII. Cross-Sectional and Radiographic Anatomy",
   "body": "*Key: ◆ = Snell's clinical notes · book only*\n\n### CROSS-SECTIONAL ANATOMY\n- Study the labelled cross sections (photographed on their inferior surfaces, so viewed from below) to help read abdominal CT scans\n- – T11 section — liver (right + left lobes), fundus of the stomach, both cupolae of the diaphragm, abdominal aorta, IVC, spinal cord. (The big pleural cavity is an embalming artifact.)\n- – L2 section — right + left kidneys, gallbladder, 2nd part of duodenum, portal vein, IVC, aorta, pancreas body, spleen, stomach body, esophagogastric junction\n- – L3 section — ascending + descending colon, 3rd part of duodenum, small-bowel coils + mesentery, psoas, cauda equina, aorta, IVC, linea alba, rectus abdominis Cross section at the body of L3, viewed from below RADIOGRAPHIC APPEARANCES OF THE ABDOMEN\n- Standard anteroposterior radiograph, supine. Examine systematically: # Structure What you see 1 Bones Lower ribs at the top; lower thoracic + lumbar vertebrae, sacrum, coccyx down the middle; sacroiliac joints, pelvic bones, hip joints each side 2 Diaphragm Dome-shaped shadows; right slightly higher than left (not seen on the book's sample film) 3 Psoas Lateral borders cast shadows passing down + laterally from T12 4 Liver Homogeneous opacity in the upper abdomen 5 Spleen Soft shadow in the left 9th and 10th intercostal spaces (not seen on the book's sample film) 6 Kidneys Usually visible: perirenal fat gives a transradiant (dark) line 7 Stomach + intestines Gas in the fundus and in the intestines; fecal material in the colon 8 Bladder Shadow in the pelvis if it contains enough urine Main features of the AP abdominal radiograph (line diagram + patient position)\n### RADIOGRAPHIC APPEARANCES OF THE GI TRACT\nRegion Appearance (barium) Stomach (barium meal; watery barium sulfate) Erect: first mouthfuls form a triangular shadow, apex down; gas bubble in the fundus above the fluid level. As it fills, greater + lesser curvatures, body and pyloric parts show. Pylorus moves down to the level of L3. Fluoroscopy: peristaltic waves start near the middle of the body and pass to the pylorus; breathing displaces the fundus Duodenum Barium forms a triangular homogeneous shadow, the duodenal cap (base toward the pylorus). 1st part smooth (no mucosal folds); the rest looks flocculent because plicae circulares break up the barium. Barium leaves the cap quickly by peristalsis Jejunum + ileum Barium enters the jejunum in minutes, reaches the ileocecal junction in 30 min – 2 h; most has left the small bowel by 6 h. Jejunum + upper ileum: mucosal folds + peristalsis scatter the shadow; last ileum: a continuous mass Large intestine Barium enema (better than a meal): ~1 L (2–3 pints) through the anal canal; whole outline seen on AP; oblique/lateral views for the flexures. Typical sacculations when filled; mucosal pattern after evacuation. The appendix often fills. Contrast (double) enema = air introduced after evacuating most barium Barium meal — main features of the stomach + duodenum (air-filled fundus, lesser curvature, pylorus, DJ flexure) Small intestine after a barium meal Large intestine after a barium enema Contrast enema — air added after evacuating most barium\n- Arteriography — a catheter goes into the femoral artery, up the external + common iliac and aorta under direct vision on a screen to the opening of the wanted artery; radiopaque material is injected (example: superior mesenteric arteriogram, below) SMA arteriogram: catheter via the right femoral → iliacs → aorta → SMA origin; middle colic, right colic, ileocolic and jejunal branches RADIOGRAPHIC APPEARANCES OF THE BILIARY DUCTS AND URINARY TRACT\n- Biliary ducts — normally not visible on a plain film. An iodine-containing compound (oral or by injection) is absorbed, excreted in bile and concentrated in the gallbladder → pear-shaped opacity in the angle between the right 12th rib and the vertebral column\n- – After a fatty meal the gallbladder contracts and the cystic + bile ducts appear as the medium passes to the 2nd part of the duodenum\n- – Sonogram can also show the gallbladder lumen\n- Kidneys — usually visible on a plain film (perirenal fat = transradiant line). Calyces, renal pelvis + ureter are not visible without contrast Method How What you see Intravenous pyelography Iodine compound injected into a subcutaneous arm vein; excreted + concentrated by the kidneys Calyces, pelvis, ureter opaque; bladder when enough is excreted. Ureters superimposed on the lumbar transverse processes → cross the sacroiliac joints → turn medially near the ischial spines → bladder. The 3 normal ureteric constrictions can be recognized Retrograde pyelography Cystoscope through the urethra into the bladder; ureteric catheter; sodium iodide solution injected up the ureter When the minor calyces fill: detail of minor + major calyces + pelvis; each minor calyx is cup-shaped because the renal papilla projects into it Gallbladder after an iodine-containing compound — 11th rib, gallbladder, intestinal gas Ureter + renal pelvis after IV injection of iodine compound Main features of the pyelogram Both kidneys 15 min after IV contrast (5-year-old girl) — calyces, pelvis and upper ureters; positions of the suprarenals, spleen, liver margin\n- Surface anatomy of the abdominal viscera — see VI. Surface Anatomy earlier in these notes",
