   "id": "n-ga-055",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Pelvis & Gluteal · IV. Arteries & Veins of the Pelvis & Gluteal Region",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture audio add-ons · plain bullets = study guide, word for word*\n\n### 1. OVERVIEW OF PELVIC ARTERIAL SUPPLY\n- Major Inflow Pathways: Arterial supply to the pelvic walls, viscera, perineum, and gluteal region is derived primarily from the internal iliac arteries, supplemented by the superior rectal, ovarian, and median sacral arteries. Origin and Division of Main Trunks Common Iliac Artery Terminates at the pelvic inlet anterior to the sacroiliac joint by dividing into the external iliac artery and internal iliac artery. External Iliac Artery Follows the pelvic brim along the medial border of the psoas muscle, gives off the inferior epigastric and deep circumflex iliac branches, and exits the false pelvis deep to the inguinal ligament to continue as the femoral artery. Internal Iliac Artery Passes into the true pelvis down to the upper margin of the greater sciatic foramen, where it splits into an anterior division and a posterior division.\n- Anatomical Variability: Although terminal branches exhibit frequent variation in their origins, their destinations remain constant; identifying individual vessels by their target territories is more reliable than by their branching origin.\n### 2. BRANCHES OF THE INTERNAL ILIAC ARTERY\nA. Anterior Division Branches Branch Course & Distribution Umbilical Artery The proximal patent portion gives rise to the superior vesical artery, which supplies the upper aspect of the urinary bladder and gives off the artery to the vas deferens in males. Obturator Artery Runs forward along the lateral pelvic wall alongside the obturator nerve. Leaves the pelvis via the obturator canal to supply the adductor compartment of the thigh. Inferior Vesical Artery (Male) Supplies the base of the bladder, the prostate gland, and the seminal vesicles. Vaginal Artery (Female) Usually replaces the inferior vesical artery; supplies the vagina and the base of the bladder. Uterine Artery (Female) Runs medially along the pelvic floor and crosses superior to the ureter. Passes above the lateral vaginal fornix, ascends between the layers of the broad ligament along the lateral border of the uterus, and ends by anastomosing with the ovarian artery along the uterine tube; provides a vaginal branch. Middle Rectal Artery Often arises in common with the inferior vesical artery. Supplies the muscular wall of the lower rectum and forms anastomoses with the superior and inferior rectal arteries. Internal Pudendal Artery (Pelvic & Gluteal Gateway) Exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen inferior to the piriformis muscle into the gluteal region. Curls around the ischial spine (accompanied by the pudendal nerve) and enters the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen. Courses forward within the pudendal canal to supply the anal canal musculature, perineal skin, and muscles. Branch Course & Distribution Inferior Gluteal Artery (Gluteal Gateway) Passes between the first and second (or second and third) sacral spinal nerves. Exits the pelvis via the greater sciatic foramen inferior to the piriformis muscle to supply the gluteal region. B. Posterior Division Branches Branch Course & Distribution Iliolumbar Artery Ascends across the pelvic inlet posterior to the external iliac vessels, psoas, and iliacus muscles. Lateral Sacral Arteries Descend anterior to the sacral plexus, providing branches to adjacent tissues and entering the anterior sacral foramina. Superior Gluteal Artery (Gluteal Gateway) Leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen superior to the piriformis muscle to supply the muscles of the gluteal region.\n### 3. ADDITIONAL ARTERIES ENTERING THE TRUE PELVIS\n- Superior Rectal Artery: The direct continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, changing its name as it crosses the common iliac artery. Supplies the rectal mucous membrane and the upper half of the anal canal.\n- Ovarian Artery: Arises directly from the abdominal aorta at the L1 level. Descends retroperitoneally, crosses the external iliac artery at the pelvic inlet, and travels through the suspensory ligament of the ovary and broad ligament to supply the ovary via the mesovarium. (The testicular artery traverses the inguinal canal and does not enter the pelvic cavity).\n- Median Sacral Artery: A small branch originating at the bifurcation of the abdominal aorta, descending in the midline over the anterior surfaces of the sacrum and coccyx.\n### 4. VENOUS DRAINAGE OF THE PELVIS AND GLUTEAL REGION\nVenous patterns generally run parallel to the arterial distribution: Vessel Formation / Course Internal Iliac Vein Formed by the union of tributaries corresponding to the branches of the internal iliac artery (including gluteal, obturator, pudendal, and visceral veins). Ascends in front of the sacroiliac joint to unite with the external iliac vein, forming the common iliac vein. External Iliac Vein Continuation of the femoral vein beginning posterior to the inguinal ligament. Courses along the medial side of the external iliac artery, receives the inferior epigastric and deep circumflex iliac veins, and joins the internal iliac vein. Median Sacral Veins Accompany the median sacral artery over the anterior surface of the sacrum and empty directly into the left common iliac vein. Pelvic Lymphatics Lymph node chains follow these major venous and arterial pathways and are designated accordingly as external iliac, internal iliac, and common iliac nodes.\n### 5. SUMMARY TABLE: GLUTEAL & PELVIC NEUROVASCULAR GATEWAYS\nVessel Primary Origin / Drainage Exit / Entry Gateway Position Relative to Piriformis Primary Distribution Superior Gluteal Artery & Vein Posterior division of internal iliac vessels Greater sciatic foramen Superior to piriformis Gluteal musculature Inferior Gluteal Artery & Vein Anterior division of internal iliac vessels Greater sciatic foramen Inferior to piriformis Gluteal musculature & upper posterior thigh Internal Pudendal Artery & Vein Anterior division of internal iliac vessels Greater sciatic foramen → curls around ischial spine → lesser sciatic foramen Inferior to piriformis Gluteal transition into pudendal canal; perineum, anal canal, external genitalia Obturator Artery & Vein Anterior division of internal iliac vessels Obturator canal N/A (lateral pelvic wall) Adductor compartment of the thigh\n### 6. APPLIED & CLINICAL ANATOMY\n- Severe Retroperitoneal Hemorrhage in Pelvic Fractures:\n- – The large, thin-walled internal iliac veins and their tributaries lie embedded within the parietal pelvic fascia beneath the parietal peritoneum.\n- – Disruptions of the rigid pelvic ring or sacral fractures easily shear and lacerate these veins, resulting in massive, concealed retroperitoneal blood accumulation and life-threatening hypovolemic shock without external signs of hemorrhage.\n- Ureteral Relationship with the Uterine Artery:\n- – The uterine artery crosses superior to the ureter near the lateral fornix of the vagina (“water under the bridge”), representing a critical site of surgical vulnerability during pelvic procedures. Slide: arteries of the pelvis LECTURE\n- ★ Abdominal aorta → right + left common iliac → internal + external iliac → femoral artery and vein.\n- ★ The thin internal iliac veins tear in pelvic fractures → concealed bleeding. The uterine artery crosses above the ureter (“water under the bridge”).",
