   "title": "Pelvis & Gluteal · V. Surface Anatomy & Palpation (Lecture + Slides)",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = lecture audio add-ons · plain bullets = study guide, word for word*\n\n### LECTURE\n- ★ Know how to palpate: iliac crest, ASIS, PSIS, ischial tuberosity, greater trochanter, symphysis pubis. Safe injection site: upper lateral quadrant of the buttock.\n- ★ Locate the sciatic nerve with imaginary lines: find the ischial tuberosity and greater trochanter → horizontal line → vertical line at 90° → another horizontal line at S3 (spinous process). The nerve runs through the middle portion of that space; if pressing there makes the numbness worse, something is wrong with the sciatic nerve.\n- ★ Is the pelvis level? Standing patient: thumb on the ASIS, middle fingers on the PSIS; ASISs and PSISs should be symmetrical (check front and side).\n- – asymmetry may mean poor posture: anterior tilt → ↑ lumbar lordosis; posterior tilt → flat lumbar spine (sway-back / flat-back posture) Slide: buttock quadrants — safe injection site Slide: sciatic nerve, gluteal region Slide: surface anatomy, posterior view (sacral dimple = PSIS) Slide: surface anatomy, anterior view",
