   "id": "n-ga-058",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Thigh · I. Bones of the Thigh — Femur, Angle & Fractures",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = Dr. Klein Lim's lecture · plain bullets = Snell Ch. 10*\n\n### HIP BONE & FEMUR\n- ★ Unlike the upper limb (mobility, reaching), the lower limb is built for weight bearing + locomotion. The two hip bones meet the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint (back) and each other at the symphysis pubis (front).\n- ★ Hip bone (os coxae) = ilium + ischium + pubis, fused at the Y-shaped triradiate cartilage inside the acetabulum.\n- ★ LECTURE TRICK Three-in-one socket = ilium + ischium + pubis meeting at the acetabulum. Fig 10.9 · Muscles attached to the outer hip bone + the back of the femur. Fig 10.10–10.11 · Femur, anterior + posterior views, with muscle attachments.\n- Organization of the lower limb: gluteal region, thigh, knee, leg, ankle + foot. The thigh and the leg are compartmentalized — each compartment has its own muscles (group function) and its own distinct nerve + blood supply. Main function: support the weight of the body + a stable foundation for standing, walking + running.\n- Hip bone, outer surface: ilium (upper flattened part) — iliac crest → ASIS (front) + PSIS (back) · iliac tubercle ≈ 5 cm (2 in.) behind the ASIS · anterior + posterior inferior iliac spines below them · greater sciatic notch. Ischium (L-shaped: body + ramus) — ischial spine · ischial tuberosity. Pubis — body, superior ramus, inferior ramus · pubic crest → pubic tubercle · symphysis pubis. The obturator foramen is filled in by the obturator membrane.\n- Acetabulum — deep depression on the outer surface of the hip bone; articulates with the almost spherical head of the femur (hip joint). Its articular surface is a horseshoe-shaped area covered with hyaline cartilage; the deficient lower margin = acetabular notch; the nonarticular floor = acetabular fossa. In the anatomic position the front of the symphysis pubis + the two ASISs lie in the same vertical plane.\n- Femur — articulates above with the acetabulum (hip joint) and below with the tibia + patella (knee joint).\n- Upper end — head (≈ ⅔ of a sphere) · neck · greater + lesser trochanters. The fovea capitis (pit in the head) holds the ligament of the head. The neck passes downward, backward + laterally.\n- The head of the femur (the part not inside the acetabulum) can be palpated on the front of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament and just lateral to the femoral pulse; tenderness there usually means arthritis of the hip joint.\n- Trochanters are joined by the intertrochanteric line (front; iliofemoral ligament) and the intertrochanteric crest (back; has the quadrate tubercle).\n- Shaft — smooth in front; behind is the linea aspera (muscles + intermuscular septa). Its margins diverge: the medial one → medial supracondylar ridge → adductor tubercle; the lateral one → lateral supracondylar ridge. The gluteal tuberosity lies below the greater trochanter. Lower end of the shaft: flat triangular popliteal surface.\n- Lower end — lateral + medial condyles (separated behind by the intercondylar notch; joined in front by the patellar surface) · epicondyles above them. The adductor tubercle is continuous with the medial epicondyle.\n### NECK–SHAFT ANGLE\n- The neck makes an angle of ≈ 125° with the shaft in the adult (slightly less in the female); ≈ 160° in a young child. Disease can change it.\n### COXA VALGA COXA VARA\nAngle increased decreased Seen in congenital dislocation of the hip fracture of the femoral neck · slipping of the femoral epiphysis Limits adduction of the hip abduction of the hip\n- ★ LECTURE TRICK Vara is very close = angle ↓, knees together. Valga is very away = angle ↑, knees far apart.\n- ★ Shenton’s line — a smooth, continuous curve along the inferior border of the pubic ramus and the medial aspect of the femoral neck (hip radiograph). Disrupted line → femoral neck fracture or developmental dysplasia of the hip.\n- On the AP hip radiograph the inferior margin of the femoral neck should form a continuous curve with the upper margin of the obturator foramen (Shenton’s line) — a useful way to assess the angle of the femoral neck.\n- ★ PT/OT: abnormal neck–shaft angles change the mechanical advantage of the hip abductors → compensatory gait deviation in the stance phase. Fig 10.70 · Bilateral congenital dislocation of the hip.\n- Congenital dislocation of the hip (the lecture’s developmental dysplasia of the hip) — 10 × more common in girls, particularly common in northern Italy. Three possible causes: generalized joint laxity · breech position (flexed hip + extended knees alter the pressure of the head on the acetabulum → the upper part of the acetabulum fails to develop) · shallow acetabulum (runs in families).\n- The upper lip of the acetabulum fails to develop, so the head rides up out of the acetabulum onto the gluteal surface of the ilium. Diagnose at birth; treat by splinting the joint in abduction.\n### BLOOD SUPPLY OF THE FEMORAL HEAD & NECK\n- Ligament of the head — a small branch of the obturator artery runs along it and enters at the fovea (the epiphysis of the head in the young).\n- The upper neck gets a profuse supply from the medial femoral circumflex artery; its branches pierce the capsule and ascend the neck deep to the synovial membrane. While the epiphyseal cartilage remains, the two sources do not communicate; in the adult an anastomosis forms.\n- ★ The retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery are the primary supply of the head + neck. The ligament’s branch alone is insufficient in adults.\n- A neck fracture interrupts the supply from the root of the neck → the scant ligament flow cannot keep the head alive → ischemic (avascular) necrosis.\n- ★ High-yield: main blood supply of the femoral head = medial femoral circumflex artery. Fig 10.72 · Hip radiograph, Shenton’s line. Fig 10.18B · Arterial supply of the femoral head.\n### FRACTURES\nSUBCAPITAL (neck) TROCHANTERIC (neck) Capsule intracapsular extracapsular — ★ intertrochanteric or subtrochanteric Who elderly, after a minor trip or stumble; esp. postmenopausal women — thinner cortical + trabecular bone from estrogen deficiency (osteoporosis) young + middle-aged; direct trauma Blood supply retinacular vessels torn → avascular necrosis (common complication) both fragments have a profuse blood supply\n- Why shortened: rectus femoris, adductors + hamstrings pull the distal fragment up (shortening is measured from the ASIS to the adductor tubercle or medial malleolus). Why laterally rotated: gluteus maximus, piriformis, obturator internus, gemelli + quadratus femoris rotate it out → toes point laterally.\n- Shaft fractures — usually in young, healthy persons. Considerable traction on the distal fragment is usually needed to overcome the powerful muscles + restore the limb’s correct length before manipulation + operative therapy. Fig 10.12 · Neck fractures: displaced leg. Fig 10.13 · Shaft fractures (A–C).\n### THIRD OF\n### SHAFT\n### PROXIMAL FRAGMENT DISTAL FRAGMENT\nUpper flexed by iliopsoas · abducted by gluteus medius + minimus · laterally rotated by the same lateral rotators as above adducted by the adductors · pulled up by hamstrings + quadriceps · laterally rotated by adductors + weight of the foot Middle — pulled up by hamstrings + quadriceps → considerable shortening; rotated backward by the 2 heads of gastrocnemius Distal — same, but the fragment is smaller and rotated back more by gastrocnemius → may press on the popliteal artery → blood flow to leg + foot\n- ★ High-yield: unimpacted neck fracture = shortened, laterally (externally) rotated limb · distal femoral shaft fracture = popliteal artery injury (posterior displacement by gastrocnemius).\n- ★ PT/OT: patient transfers + weight-bearing precautions after hip fracture or arthroplasty depend strictly on whether the repair was intracapsular or extracapsular.",
