   "title": "Thigh · II. Skin, Fascia & Superficial Veins",
   "body": "*Key: ★ = Dr. Klein Lim's lecture · plain bullets = Snell Ch. 10*\n\n### CUTANEOUS NERVES\n### NERVE FROM SUPPLIES\nLateral cutaneous n. of thigh lumbar plexus L2, 3 skin of the lateral thigh + knee (enters behind the lateral end of the inguinal ligament) Femoral branch of genitofemoral n. + ilioinguinal n. lumbar plexus L1, 2 · L1 small skin areas just below the inguinal ligament (middle · medial part) Intermediate + medial cutaneous nn. of thigh femoral n. anterior thigh · medial thigh Anterior division of obturator n. obturator n. variable area on the medial thigh Posterior cutaneous n. of thigh sacral plexus skin of the back of the thigh + upper leg Patellar plexus lateral, intermediate + medial cutaneous nn. + infrapatellar branch of saphenous n. skin in front of the knee\n### FASCIA OF THE THIGH\n- ★ Fascia — a sheet or band of fibrous tissue deep to the skin that lines + invests (goes in and separates structures). Superficial fascia blends with the reticular layer of the dermis · deep fascia envelops + compartmentalizes muscles (sometimes bones + neurovascular structures) · visceral fascia suspends organs in their cavities — not found in the thigh.\n- Superficial fascia (subcutaneous tissue) — loose areolar + adipose tissue uniting the dermis to the deep fascia. Deep fascia — a membranous connective-tissue layer that invests the muscles + other deep structures; in the limbs it forms a definite sheath that holds them in place, and fibrous septa from its deep surface divide the limb into compartments. At joints it thickens into retinacula (hold tendons in position, act as pulleys). Knowing the deep-fascia arrangement explains the path an infection takes when it spreads from its primary site.\n- Superficial fascia: the membranous layer of the abdominal wall extends into the thigh and attaches to the fascia lata about a fingerbreadth below the inguinal ligament; the fatty layer continues down the limb without a break (important for extravasation of urine after a ruptured urethra).\n- Saphenous opening — gap in the deep fascia (fascia lata) just below the inguinal ligament, 4 cm (1.5 in.) below + lateral to the pubic tubercle. Filled by loose cribriform fascia. Transmits the great saphenous vein, small branches of the femoral artery + lymph vessels. Its lower lateral border = falciform margin. Its size + shape vary.\n- Deep fascia = fascia lata — encloses the thigh “like a trouser leg”; attached above to the pelvis + inguinal ligament.\n- Iliotibial tract (band) — lateral thickening: iliac tubercle → lateral condyle of tibia. Receives the tensor fasciae latae + most of gluteus maximus.\n- Three fascial septa pass from the fascia lata to the linea aspera → 3 compartments (anterior, medial, posterior), each with muscles, nerves + arteries. Fig 10.22 · Cross-section of the thigh.\n- ★ THE THREE COMPARTMENTS AT A GLANCE ANTERIOR MEDIAL POSTERIOR Muscles sartorius, iliacus, psoas, pectineus, quadriceps femoris gracilis, adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, obturator externus biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus (+ hamstring part of adductor magnus) Action flex hip + extend knee adduct the thigh extend hip + flex knee (always opposite hip vs knee) Nerve femoral (L2–L4) obturator (L2–L4) sciatic (L5, S1, S2) Artery femoral profunda femoris + obturator perforating branches of profunda femoris\n- ★ LECTURE TRICK “A Few Men Object to Poor Science” = Anterior–Femoral · Medial–Obturator · Posterior–Sciatic. Muscles of one compartment share the same action + the same nerve.\n### SUPERFICIAL VEINS & THE VENOUS PUMP\n- Great saphenous vein (GSV) — passes directly in front of the medial malleolus and ascends with the saphenous nerve in the superficial fascia of the medial leg; it goes through the saphenous opening to join the femoral vein. Many valves.\n### GSV PATH\nDorsal venous arch (medial end) → in front of medial malleolus → medial leg → behind knee → medial thigh → saphenous opening → femoral vein\n- At the saphenous opening it receives 3 tributaries: superficial circumflex iliac, superficial epigastric + superficial external pudendal veins (matching the 3 femoral artery branches). An accessory vein joins mid-thigh or higher. Branches behind the knee link it to the small saphenous vein (back of the lower thigh drains there).\n- Veins of the limb: superficial (great + small saphenous) · deep (venae comitantes, popliteal, femoral) · perforating (connect superficial → deep; valves block deep → superficial flow).\n- Venous pump — muscle contraction squeezes the thin-walled, valved deep veins in their closed fascial compartments and forces blood up (arterial pulsations help); as muscles relax, blood is sucked from the superficial into the deep veins. Saphenous veins lie in the superficial fascia, so they are not compressed.\n- ★ The deep veins (venae comitantes) are in rigid, unyielding compartments; walking or ankle pumps squeeze them and push blood upward. Perforators have one-way valves (superficial → deep only).\n- Varicose veins — larger, elongated, tortuous. Causes: hereditary weak walls / incompetent valves · ↑ intra-abdominal pressure (multiple pregnancies, abdominal tumors) · thrombophlebitis of the deep veins (superficial veins become the main pathway). An incompetent perforator valve lets high-pressure deep blood escape outward with every exercise. Treatment: ligate tributaries + perforators, ± strip (deep veins must be patent).\n- ★ Incompetent perforator valves → high-pressure deep blood surges back into low-pressure superficial veins → varicose veins, venous stasis + ulcers.\n- ★ PT/OT: active ankle pumps + rhythmic muscle contraction run the pump; early ambulation prevents deep vein thrombosis (DVT).\n- GSV cutdown — constant position in front of the medial malleolus (emergency transfusion; the saphenous nerve lies just in front of the vein, phlebitis is a risk). At the groin (4 cm below + lateral to the pubic tubercle) the vein is larger (large-diameter catheters, rapid infusion of large volumes) and phlebitis is rare.\n- Cutdown technique — ankle: block the saphenous nerve branches (skin in front of the medial malleolus); transverse incision across the vein just anterior + superior to the medial malleolus. Groin: block the ilioinguinal + intermediate cutaneous nerve of the thigh branches (skin below + lateral to the scrotum or labium majus); transverse incision centered ≈ 4 cm below + lateral to the pubic tubercle (if the femoral pulse is felt, the incision is carried medially to just medial of the pulse); the GSV lies in the subcutaneous fat and passes through the saphenous opening to the femoral vein (4 cm / two fingerbreadths below + lateral to the pubic tubercle).\n- The GSV is also the graft for coronary bypass (reversed so valves don’t block flow; afterward the superficial blood ascends through perforators into the deep veins) and can bypass obstructions of the brachial or femoral arteries.\n- ★ LECTURE TRICK GSV cutdown: superior and anterior to the medial malleolus. Fig 10.3 · Superficial veins, arteries + inguinal nodes; saphenous opening.",
