  {
   "id": "n-ga-082",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Lab · Pelvis, Gluteal & Thigh Moina · Anterior Thigh",
   "body": "## Landmarks to find first\n- **Inguinal ligament:** ASIS to pubic tubercle; psoas, iliacus and the femoral vessels + nerve pass under it\n- **Internal iliac artery:** pelvis, gluteal region, perineum\n- **External iliac artery:** passes under the inguinal ligament and becomes the femoral artery\n- **Femoral NAVL** (lateral to medial): Nerve · Artery · Vein · Lymphatics\n- **Femoral triangle:** inguinal ligament (top), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial); floor = pectineus + iliopsoas\n\n## The 4 proximal muscles\n- **Psoas** (deep, beside the lumbar spine) O: transverse processes, bodies, discs of T12–L5 · I: with iliacus into lesser trochanter · N: lumbar plexus · A: flexes thigh on trunk; if thigh fixed, flexes trunk (sitting up)\n- **Iliacus** (fills the iliac fossa) O: iliac fossa · I: with psoas into lesser trochanter · N: femoral nerve · A: same as psoas\n- **Pectineus** (under the vessels; floor of femoral triangle) O: superior ramus of pubis · I: upper end of linea aspera · N: femoral nerve · A: flexes and adducts thigh\n- **Sartorius** (longest strap, ASIS across the thigh lateral to medial; lateral side of femoral triangle; the S of SGT) O: ASIS · I: upper medial surface of tibial shaft · N: femoral nerve · A: flexes, abducts and laterally rotates thigh; flexes and medially rotates leg\n\n## Quadriceps: one tendon, one nerve (femoral)\nAll four join the quadriceps tendon → patella → ligamentum patellae → tibial tuberosity.\n- **Rectus femoris** (only quad from the pelvis) O: straight head AIIS; reflected head ilium above acetabulum · A: extends knee, flexes hip\n- **Vastus lateralis** O: upper end and shaft of femur · A: extends knee\n- **Vastus medialis** O: upper end and shaft of femur · A: extends knee, stabilizes patella\n- **Vastus intermedius** (under rectus femoris) O: anterior and lateral shaft of femur · A: extends knee; articularis genus retracts synovial membrane",
   "order": 82,
   "updated": 1794880000000
  },
  {
   "id": "n-ga-083",
   "subject": "gross-anatomy",
   "title": "Lab · Pelvis, Gluteal & Thigh Moina · Medial & Posterior Thigh",
   "body": "## Medial thigh: adductors & gracilis (obturator nerve)\nAdductor longus = medial edge of the femoral triangle. Brevis lies behind it; magnus is the biggest and deepest.\n- **Adductor longus** O: body of pubis · I: linea aspera · N: obturator · A: adducts thigh, assists lateral rotation\n- **Adductor brevis** O: inferior ramus of pubis · I: posterior shaft of femur · N: obturator · A: same\n- **Adductor magnus** O: inferior pubic ramus, ischial ramus, ischial tuberosity · I: posterior shaft of femur, adductor tubercle · N: adductor part obturator; hamstring part sciatic · A: adducts thigh, assists lateral rotation; hamstring part extends hip\n- **Gracilis** (long, thin, most medial; the G of SGT) O: inferior pubic ramus, ischial ramus · I: upper medial tibial shaft · N: obturator · A: adducts thigh, flexes knee\n\n> **SGT (Say Grace before Tea):** sartorius, gracilis, semitendinosus all insert on the upper medial tibial shaft, but have three nerves (femoral, obturator, tibial part of sciatic) and sit in three compartments (anterior, medial, posterior).\n\n## Posterior thigh: hamstrings (sciatic nerve)\nLateral to medial: biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus.\n- **Biceps femoris** (lateral) O: long head ischial tuberosity; short head linea aspera + lateral supracondylar ridge · I: head of fibula · N: long head tibial part, short head common peroneal part · A: flexes and laterally rotates knee; long head extends hip\n- **Semitendinosus** (the long TENdon; T of SGT) O: ischial tuberosity · I: upper medial tibial shaft · N: tibial part · A: flexes and medially rotates knee, extends hip\n- **Semimembranosus** (medial, deeper) O: ischial tuberosity · I: medial condyle of tibia · N: tibial part · A: flexes and medially rotates knee, extends hip\n\n## Also in the Moina (not on the cadaver list)\n- **Tensor fasciae latae** O: iliac crest · I: iliotibial tract · N: superior gluteal · A: assists gluteus maximus in extending the knee\n- **Obturator externus** O: outer obturator membrane, pubic and ischial rami · I: medial surface of greater trochanter · N: obturator · A: lateral rotator of thigh\n- **Popliteus** O: lateral surface of lateral femoral condyle · I: posterior tibia above soleal line · N: tibial · A: flexes knee; unlocks the knee by laterally rotating femur on tibia",
   "order": 83,
   "updated": 1794880000000
  }
